Q: How to build a controlled and uncontrolled Input component in React at the same time? A: <p>A component should not be controlled and uncontrolled at the same time. Instead, it should support both APIs and let the caller choose the mode.</p>
<p>If the component is only a wrapper around a native <code>input</code>, forwarding the props is enough because the native input already supports both modes:</p>
<pre><code>function Input(props) {
  return &lt;input {...props} /&gt;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>It can then be used as a controlled input:</p>
<pre><code>import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;

function Form() {
  const [name, setName] = useState(&quot;&quot;);

  return (
    &lt;Input
      value={name}
      onChange={(event) =&gt; setName(event.target.value)}
    /&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or as an uncontrolled input:</p>
<pre><code>function Form() {
  return &lt;Input defaultValue=&quot;John&quot; /&gt;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If the custom component needs access to its current value internally, it can keep state only when it is being used in uncontrolled mode:</p>
<pre><code>import { useRef, useState } from &quot;react&quot;;

function Input({
  value: controlledValue,
  defaultValue = &quot;&quot;,
  onChange,
  ...props
}) {
  const { current: isControlled } = useRef(
    controlledValue !== undefined
  );

  const [internalValue, setInternalValue] = useState(defaultValue);

  const value = isControlled
    ? controlledValue
    : internalValue;

  function handleChange(event) {
    if (!isControlled) {
      setInternalValue(event.target.value);
    }

    onChange?.(event);
  }

  return (
    &lt;input
      {...props}
      value={value ?? &quot;&quot;}
      onChange={handleChange}
    /&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>When <code>value</code> is provided, the parent owns the state and must update it through <code>onChange</code>. When <code>value</code> is not provided, the component manages its own state starting from <code>defaultValue</code>.</p>
<p>The mode is determined during the first render and should remain unchanged for the component’s lifetime. Switching between controlled and uncontrolled modes can produce inconsistent behavior and React warnings.</p>

Q: Trouble with loading a dynamically named library in CodeIgniter A: <p>Not sure why you're loading a model into a library.</p>

<p>Make sure your library is also stored in <code>application/libraries</code></p>

<pre><code>$this-&gt;load-&gt;library('your_library_name');
$this-&gt;your_library_name-&gt;render($page-&gt;id);
</code></pre>

<p>This is similar to loading CodeIgniter libraries.</p>

Q: Custom WinForms ComboBox Flickers in Windows 11 A: <p>The easiest solution would be to simply cease the following:</p>
<pre><code>base.WndProc(ref m);

if (m.Msg == WM_PAINT)
{
    Graphics.FromHwnd(...);
    // paint again
}
</code></pre>
<p>In your current code, you let Windows paint the ComboBox and paint over it. This is what causes the flicker on Windows 11.</p>
<p>In WinForms .NET Framework 4.8, substitute your WM_PAINT code for this one:</p>
<pre><code>private const int WM_PAINT = 0x000F;
private const int WM_ERASEBKGND = 0x0014;

protected override void WndProc(ref Message m)
{
    if (m.Msg == WM_ERASEBKGND)
    {
        // We draw the complete background ourselves.
        m.Result = (IntPtr)1;
        return;
    }

    if (m.Msg == WM_PAINT)
    {
        PAINTSTRUCT ps;
        IntPtr hdc = BeginPaint(Handle, out ps);

        try
        {
            using (Graphics g = Graphics.FromHdc(hdc))
            using (BufferedGraphics buffer =
                BufferedGraphicsManager.Current.Allocate(
                    g, ClientRectangle))
            {
                Graphics bg = buffer.Graphics;

                if (DropDownStyle == ComboBoxStyle.DropDownList)
                    DrawDropDown(bg);
                else
                    DrawComboBox(bg);

                buffer.Render(g);
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            EndPaint(Handle, ref ps);
        }

        m.Result = IntPtr.Zero;
        return;
    }

    base.WndProc(ref m);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Add these Win32 definitions:</p>
<pre><code>[StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
private struct RECT
{
    public int Left;
    public int Top;
    public int Right;
    public int Bottom;
}

[StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
private struct PAINTSTRUCT
{
    public IntPtr hdc;
    public bool fErase;
    public RECT rcPaint;
    public bool fRestore;
    public bool fIncUpdate;

    [MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.ByValArray, SizeConst = 32)]
    public byte[] rgbReserved;
}

[DllImport(&quot;user32.dll&quot;)]
private static extern IntPtr BeginPaint(
    IntPtr hWnd,
    out PAINTSTRUCT lpPaint);

[DllImport(&quot;user32.dll&quot;)]
[return: MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.Bool)]
private static extern bool EndPaint(
    IntPtr hWnd,
    ref PAINTSTRUCT lpPaint);
</code></pre>
<p>Also change your mouse code. Your current code changes <code>_isHovered</code> but doesn't explicitly repaint.</p>
<p>Use:</p>
<pre><code>protected override void OnMouseEnter(EventArgs e)
{
    base.OnMouseEnter(e);

    if (!_isHovered)
    {
        _isHovered = true;
        Invalidate();
    }
}

protected override void OnMouseLeave(EventArgs e)
{
    base.OnMouseLeave(e);

    if (_isHovered)
    {
        _isHovered = false;
        Invalidate();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Why this fixes it</h3>
<p>Before:</p>
<pre><code>Windows 11 paints ComboBox
        ↓
You paint over it
        ↓
Windows paints hover state
        ↓
You paint over it
</code></pre>
<p>Result: <strong>flicker</strong>.</p>
<p>After:</p>
<pre><code>WM_PAINT
   ↓
Draw everything into memory
   ↓
Display it once
</code></pre>
<p>Conclusion: smooth painting process.</p>
<p>You don't have to:</p>
<pre><code>ControlStyles.UserPaint
ControlStyles.OptimizedDoubleBuffer
WS_EX_COMPOSITED
</code></pre>
<p>for this approach.</p>
<p>There is another bug that needs to be addressed: whenever you receive the message for WM_CTLCOLOREDIT, you allocate a new brush each time using GDI, but the brushes are only released when the control is disposed of. This is another GDI leak, not the major flickering issue.</p>

Q: maven exec plugin :  org.apache.maven.plugin.MojoExecutionException and ClassNotFoundException A: <p>You shouldn't need to specify the -Dexec.mainClass.  You got the configuration correct, just not in the pom.xml.  Change it to the following:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;mainClass&gt;org.sample.test.ShiroDemo&lt;/mainClass&gt;
</code></pre>
